Title: ChatGPT - Electron Displacement in Bonds

URL Source: https://chatgpt.com/share/6990a870-32d0-8012-a81c-43393ae16d0e

Markdown Content:
**Electron displacement in a covalent bond** means the shared pair of electrons does **not stay exactly in the middle** between the two bonded atoms. Instead, it shifts slightly toward one atom.

### Why does this happen?

It happens because of **difference in electronegativity** (ability of an atom to attract electrons):

*   If **both atoms have equal electronegativity** → electrons stay in the middle → **non-polar covalent bond** (no displacement).

_Example: H₂, Cl₂_

*   If **one atom is more electronegative** → it pulls the shared electrons closer → **polar covalent bond** (electron displacement occurs).

_Example: HCl, H₂O_

### What results from electron displacement?

*   The atom pulling electrons becomes **slightly negative (δ−)**

*   The other atom becomes **slightly positive (δ+)**

*   This creates a **dipole (partial charge separation)**

### Simple visualization

In **H—Cl**:

 Electrons shift toward **Cl** → H(δ+) — Cl(δ−)

So, **electron displacement = unequal sharing of bonding electrons due to electronegativity difference**, leading to polarity in the covalent bond.

